The procedural definition Evaluate a validation criterion during training, retain the best parameter state, and stop after a chosen number of evaluations without sufficient improvement. The guide uses V_t<V_{\mathrm{best}}-\delta as an improvement condition for a loss to be minimized. Patience protects against small noisy reversals; the minimum improvement \delta avoids treating every tiny change as progress. The model retained should be the selected best checkpoint , not necessarily the state at the last iteration. Stopping and restoration are separate actions. The official Ignite handler demonstrates the stopping mechanism; the linked PyTorch transfer-learning tutorial demonstrates saving and restoring a best validation model. The source’s illustrative training and validation curves. The marked optimum belongs to the illustrative data; it is not a prescribed stopping epoch for other problems. Regularization · PDF p. 17 Why restricting time can restrict complexity The source describes early training as preferentially fitting stronger, better-identified directions, while later optimization can fit weaker or noisy components. Early stopping restricts the reachable solution along \theta_0\to\theta_1\to\cdots\to\theta_T. That intuition becomes concrete in linear least squares. It should not be read as a guarantee that every neural network learns “simple” semantic features first. The derivation below states the setting in which the direction-filtering picture is explicit. Expand the linear least-squares recurrence Take L(\beta)=\frac12\lVert y-X\beta\rVert^2,\qquad
\beta_{t+1}=(I-\eta X^\top X)\beta_t+\eta X^\top y, with \beta_0=0 and constant step size. In a singular direction of X=UDV^\top , let c_t be the coefficient and a_j=u_j^\top y . Then c_{t+1}=(1-\eta d_j^2)c_t+\eta d_j a_j. Summing the geometric series gives the source’s spectral expression for nonzero d_j : \beta_t=V\operatorname{diag}\left(\frac{1-(1-\eta d_j^2)^t}{d_j}\right)U^\top y. Relative to OLS, the filter is 1-(1-\eta d_j^2)^t. With a suitably small nonoscillatory step, strong singular directions approach their fitted value quickly, while weak directions remain attenuated. For example, if \eta d_j^2 is very small, the first several updates make only a small fraction of the eventual OLS adjustment. Step by step Solve gradient descent in every singular direction, step by step Setting. Use L(\beta)=\|y-X\beta\|^2/2 , constant step \eta>0 , and \beta_0=0 . Let X=U_rD_rV_r^\top contain only positive singular values d_j . These assumptions are essential: a nonzero initialization leaves an additional term. 1 · Write the affine parameter recurrence \beta_{t+1}=(I-\eta X^\top X)\beta_t+\eta X^\top y. The gradient is X^\top X\beta_t-X^\top y ; subtracting it gives the two terms above. 2 · Rotate into independent scalar recurrences Let a_t=V_r^\top\beta_t and c=U_r^\top y . Multiplying by V_r^\top gives a_{t+1,j}=(1-\eta d_j^2)a_{t,j}+\eta d_jc_j. Set q_j=1-\eta d_j^2 . Starting at zero, a_{1,j}=\eta d_jc_j,\quad a_{2,j}=\eta d_jc_j(1+q_j),\quad
 a_{t,j}=\eta d_jc_j\sum_{s=0}^{t-1}q_j^s. 3 · Evaluate the geometric sum Multiply S=1+q+\cdots+q^{t-1} by q and subtract: (1-q)S=1-q^t . Here 1-q_j=\eta d_j^2 , so a_{t,j}=\eta d_jc_j\frac{1-q_j^t}{\eta d_j^2}
=\frac{1-(1-\eta d_j^2)^t}{d_j}c_j. Consequently \beta_t=V_r\operatorname{diag}\left(\frac{1-(1-\eta d_j^2)^t}{d_j}\right)U_r^\top y. Zero-singular-value directions remain zero from this initialization; they must not be handled by literal division by zero. 4 · Distinguish stability from monotone shrinkage Convergence in every represented direction requires |1-\eta d_j^2|<1 , or 0<\eta<2/d_{\max}^2 . To have 0\le q_j<1 and filter s_t(d_j)=1-q_j^t rise monotonically from zero toward one, the stronger bound 0<\eta\le1/d_{\max}^2 is sufficient. In the larger stable interval, a filter can oscillate above and below one. 5 · Compare with Ridge quantitatively Ridge’s fitted-value factor is s_\lambda(d)=d^2/(d^2+\lambda) . Matching one direction at a time would require \lambda_j(t)=d_j^2\frac{1-s_t(d_j)}{s_t(d_j)}
=d_j^2\frac{q_j^t}{1-q_j^t}. For t>0 in the monotone regime this is nonnegative, but depends on j . A single stopping time does not generally correspond to one common Ridge penalty. The shared idea is preferential suppression of weakly identified directions, not equality of estimators. Expanded derivation Chapter source · PDF p. 16 Link to this calculation ↗ Compare with Ridge without equating them Ridge’s relative filter is d_j^2/(d_j^2+\lambda) . Finite-time descent has the exponential-in-time filter above. Both suppress poorly identified directions, but their functional forms differ. There is not generally one \lambda that makes both procedures identical in every singular direction. Expanded derivation assumption. Stability for the positive singular modes requires |1-\eta d_j^2|<1 . A smaller condition, 0<\eta d_j^2\le1 , makes the simple monotonic-filter intuition immediate. If the step is too large, oscillation or divergence invalidates the gentle “learn strong directions first” picture. Validation is not untouched testing The stopping criterion chooses a model. The validation set is consequently part of model selection. Repeatedly checking the final test set and choosing the stopping time from it makes that set part of selection too. For temporal applications, the PDF requires a time-ordered validation block and any necessary embargo. A random split can provide an attractive curve while answering the wrong deployment question. Patience does not repair a leakage-prone split. Practical interpretation A very small patience can stop on noise; a very large patience weakens the restriction. Too much combined regularization can stop the model before useful structure is learned. Interpret the curve together with capacity, optimizer behavior, and the definition of the metric. Use the existing official training-curve and checkpoint examples below. There is no custom plotting widget here: the source’s figure already conveys the concept, and the official material shows ho