Three meanings of “sparse” A parameter vector is sparse when many learned coefficients are zero. A representation is sparse when an example uses only a few latent features. A dropout mask creates temporary zeros in a computation. Similar-looking zeros do not make the mechanisms equivalent. For sparse coding, the guide writes x\approx Da , where D is a dictionary and a is the code. The code solves \hat a\in\mathop{\mathrm{argmin}}_a\left[\frac12\lVert x-Da\rVert^2+\lambda\lVert a\rVert_1\right]. The dictionary can have more columns than input dimensions; each example still uses only a small subset. This is representation sparsity , not necessarily a sparse dictionary or a sparse network parameter matrix. Rarely active hidden units A sparse autoencoder can penalize average hidden activity. If \hat\rho_j is unit j ’s observed mean activity and \rho is the desired small activity, one penalty is \sum_j\operatorname{KL}(\rho\Vert\hat\rho_j), where the Bernoulli divergence is \operatorname{KL}(\rho\Vert\hat\rho)=\rho\log\frac\rho{\hat\rho}+(1-\rho)\log\frac{1-\rho}{1-\hat\rho}. This expression assumes the activity quantities have the probability-like interpretation required by the Bernoulli divergence. It should not be applied mechanically to unrestricted hidden values. The aim is for units to be useful selectively across examples. Step by step Differentiate the sparse-activation KL penalty all the way to one activation Setting. A hidden unit has activations a_1,\ldots,a_B\in(0,1) and empirical mean \hat\rho=B^{-1}\sum_i a_i . A target activity \rho\in(0,1) is fixed. Bernoulli KL requires both probabilities to lie strictly between zero and one for this finite formula. 1 · Write the two possible Bernoulli outcomes For outcome one the probabilities are \rho and \hat\rho ; for outcome zero they are 1-\rho and 1-\hat\rho . By the definition of discrete KL, K(\hat\rho)=\rho\log\frac\rho{\hat\rho}+(1-\rho)\log\frac{1-\rho}{1-\hat\rho}. 2 · Separate constant and variable logarithms K=\rho\log\rho-\rho\log\hat\rho+(1-\rho)\log(1-\rho)-(1-\rho)\log(1-\hat\rho). Differentiate with respect to \hat\rho : K'(\hat\rho)=-\frac\rho{\hat\rho}+\frac{1-\rho}{1-\hat\rho}
=\frac{\hat\rho-\rho}{\hat\rho(1-\hat\rho)}. The denominator is positive, so the derivative points toward the target mean. 3 · Verify the minimum The derivative is zero at \hat\rho=\rho , and K''(\hat\rho)=\frac\rho{\hat\rho^2}+\frac{1-\rho}{(1-\hat\rho)^2}>0. Thus this is the unique minimum, with KL equal to zero. 4 · Continue through averaging and an activation Since \partial\hat\rho/\partial a_i=1/B , \frac{\partial K}{\partial a_i}=\frac1B\left[-\frac\rho{\hat\rho}+\frac{1-\rho}{1-\hat\rho}\right]. If a_i=\sigma(z_i) , multiply by a_i(1-a_i) to obtain \partial K/\partial z_i . A total objective weighted by \gamma multiplies this contribution by \gamma . Every example contributes to the shared mean, so this regularizer couples observations in a batch. 5 · Do not apply the probability formula blindly An unconstrained ReLU activation can exceed one, so its average is not automatically a Bernoulli probability. Use a compatible bounded activation/statistic or a different sparsity penalty. Clamping for numerical safety changes gradients at the clamped boundary and should be explicit. Expanded derivation Chapter source · PDF p. 18 Link to this calculation ↗ Dropout’s stochastic mechanism Let h be a hidden activation vector and q=1-p the keep probability, with 0<q\le1 for the scaling formula below. In inverted dropout, m_j\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(q),\qquad\tilde h=\frac{m\odot h}{q}. Conditioning on the original activation, \mathbb E[\tilde h_j\mid h_j]=h_j . Scaling during training therefore preserves the activation’s expectation, and the full activation is used without that random mask during ordinary evaluation. The guide’s example takes h=(2,1,-3,4) , p=0.5 , and mask (1,0,1,0) . The result is (4,0,-6,0) . Dropping half the entries does not mean the surviving entries retain their original value under inverted scaling. The source shows a distribution of outputs under random dropout masks alongside a deterministic evaluation output. The illustration is not a claim that the full nonlinear network exactly equals the average of all masked networks. Regularization · PDF p. 20 Step by step Derive dropout’s mean, variance, and a nonlinear counterexample Setting. Treat an activation h as fixed and draw m\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(q) , with keep probability 0<q\le1 . Inverted dropout returns \tilde h=mh/q . The case q=0 is excluded from this analytic scaling formula. 1 · Evaluate the two possible values With probability q , \tilde h=h/q . With probability 1-q , \tilde h=0 . Hence \mathbb E[\tilde h]=q\frac hq+(1-q)0=h. 2 · Calculate the second moment, then the variance \mathbb E[\tilde h^2]=q\frac{h^2}{q^2}=\frac{h^2}{q}, \operatorname{Var}(\tilde h)=\mathbb E[\tilde h^2]-\mathbb E[\tilde h]^2
=\frac{1-q}{q}h^2. At q=1/2 , the two values are 0 and 2h , with mean h and variance h^2 . 3 · Pass the noise through a linear output For independent masks m_j and s=\sum_jw_jm_jh_j/q , linearity gives \mathbb E[s]=\sum_jw_jh_j . Independence makes cross covariances zero, so \operatorname{Var}(s)=\frac{1-q}{q}\sum_jw_j^2h_j^2. For squared loss and fixed target y , \mathbb E[(s-y)^2]=(\mathbb E[s]-y)^2+\operatorname{Var}(s). In this particular linear-output case, dropout adds a feature-dependent quadratic penalty. It is not a universal single L2 coefficient for an entire nonlinear network. 4 · Show why the full deterministic network need not equal the stochastic mean Take h=1 , q=1/2 , and a following transformation f(u)=\operatorname{ReLU}(u-1) . The stochastic outputs are f(0)=0 and f(2)=1 , so \mathbb E[f(\tilde h)]=1/2 . But f(\mathbb E[\tilde h])=f(1)=0 . Preserving a layer’s activation mean does not allow expectation to pass through every later nonlinearity. 5 · Connect the identity to evaluation behavior Ordinary inverted-dropout evaluation uses the unchanged activation h instead of resampling and does not multiply it by q again. This matches the layerwise scaling convention, not an exact integration over all possible nonlinear subnetworks. Expanded derivation Chapter source · PDF p. 18 Library reference Link to this calculation ↗ Why it can regularize The guide gives three complementary intuitions. Units cannot rely on the same partners always being present; this discourages co-adaptation. Many stochastic subnetworks share parameters during training. Multiplicative noise encourages robustness to missing internal features. The expectation identity is local to the masked activation. A nonlinear downstream function generally does not commute with expectation. Treating dropout as a related-subnetwork ensemble is therefore an intuition, not an exact statement that one deterministic pass equals all-network averaging. Dropout does not erase learned weights After a masked training pass, the weights still exist. In ordinary evaluation the network uses all units. LASSO’s exact parameter zeros and a sparse code’s selective activation pattern are different from these temporary masks. The probability p is a hyperparameter, not a desired fraction of permanently removed parameters. Excessive dropout can create underfitting and noisy optimization. The PDF also cautions that placement depends on architecture: convolutional, recurrent, residual, and Transformer components have different conventions. Read train and evaluation behavior explicitly The official PyTorch MNIST example demonstrates dropout in a full classifier. The API reference documents the probability and scaling behavior. In actual code, module evaluation mode changes dropout behavior, while disabling autograd only changes graph recording. Those are not substitutes for each other. Use the model’s intended training/evaluation modes during metric calculation. A stochastic training result and a deterministic evaluation result are measuring different computations, so differences should be interpreted in that context.

Attention is one component, not the whole model A Transformer repeats attention and position-wise feed-forward blocks, with residual connections and normalization. The guide’s post-normalization encoder schematic is U=\operatorname{Norm}(X+\operatorname{MHA}(X,X,X)), Y=\operatorname{Norm}(U+\operatorname{FFN}(U)), where \operatorname{FFN}(u)=W_2\phi(W_1u+b_1)+b_2 . Attention exchanges information between positions. The feed-forward sublayer transforms each position’s representation. Residual paths preserve a direct contribution from the block input, while normalization helps manage scale across repeated transformations. The source notes that pre-normalization variants are common. The displayed equations remain the PDF’s post-normalization convention; practical implementations can arrange normalization differently. Step by step Derive LayerNorm’s statistics and complete input gradient Setting. Normalize one token’s d features x_1,\ldots,x_d . Let \mu=\frac1d\sum_i x_i,\quad v=\frac1d\sum_i(x_i-\mu)^2,\quad
r=(v+\epsilon)^{-1/2},\quad\hat x_i=(x_i-\mu)r, y_i=\gamma_i\hat x_i+\beta_i,\qquad\epsilon>0. This uses the population-form divisor d , not d-1 . The learned \gamma,\beta do not depend on the token’s computed statistics. 1 · Differentiate the mean For every input coordinate x_j , \partial\mu/\partial x_j=1/d . Consequently, \frac{\partial(x_i-\mu)}{\partial x_j}=\mathbf1_{i=j}-\frac1d. 2 · Differentiate the variance, including the mean’s dependence Apply the chain rule: \frac{\partial v}{\partial x_j}=\frac2d\sum_i(x_i-\mu)\left(\mathbf1_{i=j}-\frac1d\right). Because \sum_i(x_i-\mu)=\sum_i x_i-d\mu=0 , the mean-related sum vanishes and \frac{\partial v}{\partial x_j}=\frac2d(x_j-\mu). 3 · Differentiate inverse standard deviation \frac{\partial r}{\partial x_j}
=-\frac12(v+\epsilon)^{-3/2}\frac2d(x_j-\mu)
=-\frac{r^3}{d}(x_j-\mu). 4 · Apply the product rule to the normalized feature \frac{\partial\hat x_i}{\partial x_j}
=r\left(\mathbf1_{i=j}-\frac1d\right)-\frac{r^3}{d}(x_i-\mu)(x_j-\mu). Using (x_i-\mu)r=\hat x_i , this becomes \frac{\partial\hat x_i}{\partial x_j}
=r\left(\mathbf1_{i=j}-\frac1d-\frac{\hat x_i\hat x_j}{d}\right). The formula retains \epsilon through r and the normalized features; it does not assume their variance is exactly one. 5 · Contract the Jacobian with the upstream gradient Let g_i=\partial L/\partial y_i and a_i=g_i\gamma_i . Then \frac{\partial L}{\partial x_j}
=\sum_i a_i\frac{\partial\hat x_i}{\partial x_j}
=r\left[a_j-\frac1d\sum_i a_i-\frac{\hat x_j}{d}\sum_i a_i\hat x_i\right]. The affine gradients are \partial L/\partial\gamma_i=g_i\hat x_i and \partial L/\partial\beta_i=g_i , summed over examples or positions sharing these parameters. 6 · Compare the normalized axes, not just the names LayerNorm typically normalizes features within each token and uses those input statistics in both training and evaluation. Training BatchNorm normalizes across batch-related axes, so a prediction and its derivatives can depend on other batch members. They are not interchangeable operations merely because both subtract a mean and divide by a scale. Expanded derivation Chapter source · PDF p. 20 Library reference Link to this calculation ↗ Step by step Derive residual-path gradients and compare pre-norm with post-norm Setting. For a residual transformation y=x+F(x) , let J_F=\partial F/\partial x . Treat vectors as columns and assume differentiability at the point considered. 1 · Differentiate both additive branches The identity branch has Jacobian I . Addition sums the contributions: \frac{\partial y}{\partial x}=I+J_F. For upstream loss gradient g_y , the chain rule gives g_x=(I+J_F)^\top g_y=g_y+J_F^\top g_y. The gradient therefore has a direct path that does not traverse F . 2 · Expand two residual blocks If x_1=x_0+F_0(x_0) and x_2=x_1+F_1(x_1) , then \frac{\partial x_2}{\partial x_0}=(I+J_{F_1})(I+J_{F_0})
=I+J_{F_0}+J_{F_1}+J_{F_1}J_{F_0}. The identity term is one available path among several. The other terms can cancel or amplify it; residual connections do not prove the total gradient norm is bounded away from zero. 3 · Include normalization before the sublayer For pre-norm y=x+F(N(x)) , \frac{\partial y}{\partial x}=I+J_F(N(x))J_N(x). The direct identity path remains outside normalization. 4 · Include normalization after the residual sum For post-norm y=N(x+F(x)) , \frac{\partial y}{\partial x}=J_N(x+F(x))(I+J_F(x)). Now every path crosses the normalization Jacobian after the sum. These are distinct computations, even though both contain the same three ingredients. 5 · Avoid a universal architecture verdict This path analysis explains an optimization difference, not a theorem that one layout always wins. Initial scale, depth, initialization, normalization parameters, and optimizer settings affect the complete system. A final normalization after a stack also contributes its own Jacobian. Expanded derivation Chapter source · PDF p. 20 Link to this calculation ↗ Position must enter somewhere Without positional signals or a fixed order-dependent mask, unrestricted self-attention is permutation-equivariant: reorder the inputs and the outputs reorder correspondingly. That symmetry is appropriate for sets but does not encode sentence order or time order by itself. A fixed causal mask is already order-dependent, so that unrestricted symmetry argument cannot be applied unchanged to every masked architecture. The source gives sinusoidal positions: PE(pos,2i)=\sin\left(\frac{pos}{10000^{2i/d_{\mathrm{model}}}}\right), PE(pos,2i+1)=\cos\left(\frac{pos}{10000^{2i/d_{\mathrm{model}}}}\right). Different dimensions vary at different frequencies. The position representation can be combined with token representations so attention can distinguish order. The guide also mentions learned, relative, and rotary approaches, without treating the sinusoidal form as a universal requirement. The source’s sinusoidal position illustration. It shows how multiple frequencies encode position; it is not a learned attention map. Architectures · PDF p. 20 Step by step Prove attention equivariance and derive a sinusoidal position shift Setting. First consider self-attention without positional features, a fixed order-dependent mask, or stochastic dropout. Let P be a permutation matrix, so P^\top P=I and PX reorders token rows. 1 · Follow the permutation through the projections For input X'=PX , shared linear projections give Q'=PXW_Q=PQ , K'=PK , and V'=PV . Thus the score matrix transforms as A'=\frac{Q'K'^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}}=\frac{PQK^\top P^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}}=PAP^\top. 2 · Follow it through rowwise normalization Permuting a row’s entries permutes its exponentials but does not change their sum. Permuting rows simply reorders which row is normalized. Therefore \operatorname{softmax}_{\mathrm{row}}(PAP^\top)=P\operatorname{softmax}_{\mathrm{row}}(A)P^\top. 3 · Complete the attention operation Writing S=\operatorname{softmax}_{\mathrm{row}}(A) , O'=PSP^\top PV=PSV=PO. The output permutes in the same way as the input: equivariance , not invariance. A shared per-token feed-forward function preserves this property; a symmetric pooling over tokens can turn it into invariance for a global prediction. 4 · Identify what breaks the premise A fixed triangular causal mask is not generally unchanged by an arbitrary permutation, so the preceding unrestricted-attention symmetry proof does not apply to that fixed-mask computation. Position embeddings, relative-position biases, recurrence, or masking can all supply order-dependent structure. Positional encoding is a common mechanism, not the only possible one. 5 · Derive how one sinusoidal pair changes with position For frequency \omega , define v(p)=(\sin(\omega p),\cos(\omega p))^\top . Angle addition gives \sin[\omega(p+k)]=\sin(\omega p)\cos(\omega k)+\cos(\omega p)\sin(\omega k), \cos[\omega(p+k)]=\cos(\omega p)\cos(\omega k)-\sin(\omega p)\sin(\omega k). Consequently v(p+k)=
\begin{bmatrix}\cos(\omega k)&\sin(\omega k)\\-\sin(\omega k)&\cos(\omega k)\end{bmatrix}v(p). For a fixed offset k , this is the same linear transformation at every p . Using multiple frequencies gives several position scales. This algebra explains structure available to the model; it does not guarantee that a trained model extrapolates correctly to longer sequences. Expanded derivation Chapter source · PDF p. 20 Research reference Link to this calculation ↗ Encoder and decoder visibility An encoder normally lets input positions use bidirectional context. A causal decoder restricts each position to allowed prior context. An encoder–decoder decoder combines masked self-attention with cross-attention to the encoder’s source memory. An additive causal mask is M_{ij}=\begin{cases}0&j\le i\\-\infty&j>i.\end{cases} Adding it before softmax gives zero weight to disallowed future positions. This is a mathematical additive-mask convention. Boolean mask meanings differ across APIs: scaled_dot_product_attention uses True for an allowed entry, while MultiheadAttention boolean masks use True for a blocked entry. Every query needs a valid normalization domain; an entirely masked row is not a probability distribution under the mathematical softmax formula. In the functional SDPA call, a nonzero dropout_p applies dropout even if a surrounding module is in evaluation mode, so pass zero explicitly for deterministic evaluation. Work through the source’s dimensions With batch size B=16 , length T=100 , model width 256, and eight heads, each head can have key/value width 32. The hidden tensor is 16\times100\times256 . Per head, Q,K,V have shape 16\times100\times32 , and the score matrix is 16\times100\times100 . Across eight heads, that is 8\times16\times100\times100=1{,}280{,}000 attention weights in the naive explicit representation. This calculation explains where the sequence-length cost arises. Optimized kernels can change memory materialization and constants without turning the conceptual dense interaction pattern into recurrence. Step by step Count tensor elements, matrix products, and projection parameters Setting. Use batch B=16 , length T=100 , model width d=256 , and H=8 heads. Split widths evenly so d_k=d_v=d/H=32 . Ignore padding and describe an ordinary dense attention implementation. 1 · Follow each shape The model representation is (B,T,d)=(16,100,256) . Each head uses Q_h,K_h,V_h with shape (16,100,32) . Multiplying Q_hK_h^\top gives (16,100,100) scores. Across heads, the score count is BHT^2=16\cdot8\cdot100\cdot100=1{,}280{,}000. At four bytes per scalar, one such tensor alone occupies 5{,}120{,}000 bytes, before gradients, probabilities, or other activations. 2 · Count pairwise arithmetic For one head, computing Q_hK_h^\top needs BT^2d_k multiply–accumulate terms. Summing over heads gives BT^2Hd_k=BT^2d . Multiplying attention weights by values gives another BT^2d term. This is the origin of the O(BT^2d) interaction cost. 3 · Include the projections that the interaction shorthand omits Each of the three input projections maps d to d for every token, costing BTd^2 terms. The output projection adds one more, for roughly 4BTd^2 projection terms. Thus full attention arithmetic includes both BTd^2 and BT^2d contributions, before the feed-forward sublayer. 4 · Count trainable parameters separately Three dense d\times d input projections plus one output projection have 4d^2 weights. With one bias per projected feature in each map, add 4d biases. For d=256 , that is 262{,}144+1{,}024=263{,}168 parameters. This count does not multiply by T , B , or H when total projected width remains d . 5 · Distinguish arithmetic from materialized memory A straightforward implementation stores the quadratic score/probability arrays. Memory-efficient fused attention can avoid materializing the complete matrices while computing equivalent dense attention, so the naive storage count is not a lower bound for every implementation. The quadratic all-pairs interaction arithmetic remains distinct from this memory optimization. Actual runtime also depends on kernels, hardware, masks, and sequence lengths. Expanded derivation Chapter source · PDF p. 20 Library reference Link to this calculation ↗ Parallel work and quadratic interactions An RNN’s h_t depends on h_{t-1} , creating a sequential chain. Self-attention computes all allowed query/key interactions within a layer in parallel. Dense attention’s interaction term is O(T^2d) , while a recurrent layer has roughly O(Td_h^2) arithmetic and sequential depth O(T) in the guide’s simplified comparison. The source contrasts linear recurrent step count with quadratic dense interactions. This is a scaling illustration; constants, projections, and hardware determine real runtimes. Architectures · PDF p. 22 Long context can therefore make dense attention expensive despite its parallelism. The source motivates sparse, local, linearized, or chunked variants from this cost, rather than claiming every attention architecture has the same implementation behavior. Training and generation are different computations During teacher-forced causal training, all target positions are known, so a mask permits parallel loss calculation without future leakage. During generation, new tokens must be produced before they can become inputs to later predictions. Generation remains sequential at the token level. Key/value caching avoids recomputing earlier projections each time. It reduces repeated work, not the dependency on previously generated tokens. The official SDPA and Transformer-building-blocks tutorials illustrate actual implementations; the latter is an advanced performance-oriented resource, not the required first reading. The conceptual summary is a repeated routing-and-transformation block with explicit position and visibility rules—not merely an attention layer with more parameters.
